WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.160
The term deep learning refers to training neural networks, sometimes very large neural networks.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.480
So what exactly is a neural network?

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.040
In this video, let's try to give you some of the basic intuitions.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.720
Let's start with a housing price prediction example.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.600
Let's say you have a data set with six houses, so you know the size of the houses in square

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.600
feet or square meters, and you know the price of the house, and you want to fit a function

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.720
to predict the price of a house as a function of its size.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.240
So if you're familiar with linear regression, you might say, well, let's fit a straight line

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.240
to this data.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.480
So maybe you get a straight line like that.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.320
But to be a bit fancier, you might say, well, we know that prices can never be negative,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.320
right?

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.680
So instead of a straight line fit which will eventually become negative, let's bend the

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.680
curve here, so it just ends up zero here.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.280
So this thick blue line ends up being your function for predicting the price of a house

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.000
as a function of its size, whereas zero here and then there's a straight line fit to the

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.520
right.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:10.440
So you can think of this function that you've just fit to housing prices as a very simple

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.440
neural network.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.320
It's almost the simplest possible neural network.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.280
Let me draw it here.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.140
We have as the input to the neural network the size of a house, which we call x.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:32.260
It goes into this little circle and then it outputs the price we'll call y.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:40.540
So this little circle, which is a single neuron in our neural network, implements this function

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.440
that we drew on the left.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.100
And all that the neuron does is it inputs the size, computes this linear function,

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.360
takes a max of zero and then outputs the estimated price.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.360
And by the way, in the neural network literature, you see this function a lot.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:03.680
This function which goes at zero at some time and then takes off as a straight line.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:15.730
This function is called a RELU function, which stands for Rectified Linear Unit.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:16.730
So R-E-L-U.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:22.190
And rectified just means taking a max of zero, which is why you get a function shaped like

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.690
this.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.910
You don't need to worry about RELU units for now, but it's just something you see again

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.950
later in this course.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:37.850
So if this is a single neuron, neural network, really a tiny little neural network, a larger

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:44.630
neural network is then formed by taking many of these single neurons and stacking them together.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:51.230
So if you think of this neuron as being like a single Lego break, you then get a bigger

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.370
neural network by stacking together many of these Lego breaks.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.450
Let's see an example.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:03.310
Let's say that instead of predicting the price of a house just from its size, you now have

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.390
other features.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:09.070
You know other things about the house, such as the number of bedrooms, which we only

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.270
write as pound bedrooms.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:17.630
You might think that one of the things that really affects the price of a house is family

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:18.630
size.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.510
So can this house fit your family of three, your family of four, your family of five?

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.110
And it's really based on the size and square feet or square meters and the number of

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:33.210
bedrooms that determines whether or not a house can fit your family's family size.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:37.750
And then maybe you know the zip code in different countries.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:42.910
It's called the postal code of the house.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:48.950
And the zip code maybe is a feature that tells you walkability.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.710
So is this neighborhood highly walkable?

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.470
You can just walk to the grocery store and walk to school.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.470
Do you need to drive?

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.350
Some people prefer highly walkable neighborhoods.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:06.710
And then the zip code as well as the wealth maybe tells you, right, certainly in the

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:12.030
United States, but some other countries as well, tells you how good is the school

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.030
quality.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:19.270
So each of these little circles I'm drawing can be one of those relu rectified

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:22.790
linear units or some other slightly nonlinear function.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:26.590
So that based on the size and number of bedrooms, you can estimate the family

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.430
size, based on zip code, based on walkability, based on zip code and wealth,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.150
you can estimate the school quality.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:35.310
And then finally, you might think that while the way people decide how much

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.910
they're willing to pay for a house is they look at the things that really matter to them,

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:44.750
in this case, family size, walkability, and school quality, and that helps you predict

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.430
the price.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:56.870
So in this example, X is all of these four inputs and Y is the price you're trying

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.070
to predict.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:03.390
And so by stacking together a few of the single neurons or the simple predictors we

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:07.390
had from the previous slide, we now have a slightly larger neural network.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:12.510
How the magic of a neural network is that when you implement it, you need to give it

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:20.550
just the input X and the output Y for a number of examples in your training set,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:25.270
and all these things in the middle, it will figure out by itself.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:30.870
So what you actually implement is this, where here you have a neural network with

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:31.870
four inputs.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:37.910
So the input features might be the size, number of bedrooms, the zip code or postal code,

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:40.430
and the wealth of the neighborhood.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:48.870
And so given these input features, the job of the neural network will be to predict the

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.390
price Y.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:56.150
And notice also that each of these circles, these are called hidden units in the neural

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:02.390
network, that each of them takes its inputs, all four input features.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:09.590
So for example, rather than saying this first node represents family size, and family size

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:12.630
depends only on the features X1 and X2.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.830
Instead, we're going to say, well, neural network, you decide whatever you want this

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:21.190
node to be, and we'll give you all four input features to compute whatever you want.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:27.190
So we say that the layers, that is this input layer and this layer in the middle of the

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:31.710
neural network are densely connected, because every input feature is connected to every one

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.030
of these circles in the middle.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:39.750
And the remarkable thing about neural networks is that given enough data about X and Y, given

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:44.590
enough training examples with both X and Y, neural networks are remarkably good at figuring

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:49.070
out functions that accurately map from X to Y.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:51.750
So that's a basic neural network.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:55.190
It turns out that as you build out your own neural networks, you probably find them to

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:59.850
be most useful, most powerful in supervised learning settings, meaning that you're trying

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:05.790
to take an input X and map it to some output Y, like we just saw in the housing price prediction

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:06.790
example.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:12.190
In the next video, let's go over some more examples of supervised learning and some examples

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:16.310
of where you might find neural networks to be incredibly helpful for your applications

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:16.670
as well.
